{
  "note": "REM versus Slow Wave Sleep",
  "source": "8a3626b356c7",
  "script": "0658e6c019b3",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "c4ac03e542d1",
  "duration": 102.766,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "REM versus Slow Wave Sleep",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Sleep comes in two contrasting states.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 2.72
        },
        {
          "text": "The first is rapid eye movement sleep, known as REM sleep, and the second is slow wave sleep.",
          "start": 2.96,
          "end": 9.28
        },
        {
          "text": "The two are defined by their signatures on the electroencephalogram, and by their opposite physiological profiles.",
          "start": 9.76,
          "end": 16.4
        },
        {
          "text": "Slow wave sleep is a state of bodily rest, with a regular autonomic rhythm.",
          "start": 16.72,
          "end": 21.84
        },
        {
          "text": "REM sleep, on the other hand, combines muscle paralysis with autonomic activation.",
          "start": 21.92,
          "end": 27.2
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll take slow wave sleep first, and then turn to REM sleep.",
          "start": 27.24,
          "end": 31.12
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Slow Wave Sleep",
      "start": 31.974,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "During slow wave sleep, a substantial amount of muscle tone remains, although that tone is reduced compared with wakefulness.",
          "start": 31.974,
          "end": 40.094
        },
        {
          "text": "Body movements also remain frequent.",
          "start": 40.134,
          "end": 42.814
        },
        {
          "text": "Heart rate, blood pressure and respiration are all maintained at a regular rate.",
          "start": 42.854,
          "end": 48.094
        },
        {
          "text": "In other words, autonomic activity stays steady throughout this stage.",
          "start": 48.134,
          "end": 52.894
        },
        {
          "text": "As for the stages involved, only N three, the deepest of the three non-REM stages, is considered slow wave sleep.",
          "start": 52.934,
          "end": 61.094
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "REM Sleep",
      "start": 61.936,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "REM sleep looks very different, starting with a pronounced loss of muscle tone.",
          "start": 61.936,
          "end": 67.456
        },
        {
          "text": "As a result, the body is effectively paralysed.",
          "start": 67.536,
          "end": 71.136
        },
        {
          "text": "Heart rate, blood pressure and respiration show sharp fluctuations, rather than a regular rhythm.",
          "start": 71.216,
          "end": 77.496
        },
        {
          "text": "The rapid eye movements themselves are what give this stage its name.",
          "start": 77.536,
          "end": 81.656
        },
        {
          "text": "Brain temperature is raised as well, compared with slow wave sleep.",
          "start": 81.696,
          "end": 85.656
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, it has been suggested that dreams might reflect exactly those sharp autonomic fluctuations.",
          "start": 85.696,
          "end": 92.536
        },
        {
          "text": "So slow wave sleep pairs a resting body with a steady autonomic rhythm, while REM sleep pairs a paralysed body with fluctuating autonomic activity.",
          "start": 92.576,
          "end": 102.816
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
